<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicolas Perrenot de Granvelle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1486">1486</a>–1550</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicolas Perrenot de Granvelle was a lawyer from Franche-Comté who rose to become one of the chief ministers of the Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, serving as his keeper of the seals and principal adviser on imperial affairs from the 1530s. He built the Palais Granvelle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besancon">Besançon</a>, now a museum. His son Antoine Perrenot de Granvelle became a cardinal and a leading minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicolas-perrenot-de-granvelle.jpg" alt="Nicolas Perrenot de Granvelle" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Titian · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tizian-Nicolas_Granvelle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nicolas Perrenot de Granvelle was born at Ornans in 1486, into a family of modest standing in the Franche-Comté. In 1513 he married Nicole de Bonvalot, daughter of Jacques de Bonvalot, lord of Champagney; the couple had 15 children. He became a lawyer and was called to the Parlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dole">Dole</a> before entering the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, who was elected emperor in 1519 at the age of 19.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Granvelle quickly became one of the emperor's trusted advisers, and his sons and sons-in-law went on to hold prominent posts at the imperial court. In 1527 he bought the seigniory of Grandvelle in the Bailiwick of Amont, in what is now the Haute-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> department, whose name, later spelt Granvelle, he carried. After the death of Mercurino Gattinara in 1530 he became one of the emperor's most trusted advisers on the affairs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, and he kept that role for the rest of his life. He was also made suzerain of the imperial city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besancon">Besançon</a> and held an influential position in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Granvelle died on 27 August 1550, aged 64, while attending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>. He had gathered a significant art collection and a library, which is now in the Bibliothèque municipale de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besancon">Besançon</a>. Four of his children played prominent political roles: Antoine Perrenot de Granvelle, Archbishop of Mechelen and cardinal; Thomas Perrenot de Granvelle, Spanish ambassador in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>; Jérôme de Perrenot, a diplomat for Ferdinand I and guardian of the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a>; and Frédéric Perrenot de Champagney, a soldier and diplomat for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. Within two generations the family had become the most powerful and the richest in the Franche-Comté, a rise of which the Granvelle Palace in Besançon is the symbol.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nicolas Perrenot de Granvelle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 16th century <b>Nicolas Perrenot de Granvelle</b>, chancellor of Charles V, built his palace there, and the city was closely tied to the Habsburgs.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-city-of-besancon">Imperial City of Besançon</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.37,10.9&amp;year=1527" aria-label="Augsburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4803 472 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Nicolas Perrenot de Granvelle"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5253 742h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5253 742h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=48.37,10.9&amp;year=1527">What was here in 1527? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
